Make perl's LDLIBPTH additive on Haiku.

Haiku's runtime_loader *replaces* the default library search path with
LIBRARY_PATH whenever that variable is set -- it returns the value verbatim and
never appends the defaults (src/system/runtime_loader/runtime_loader.cpp, which
carries an upstream TODO about exactly this).

perl's generated Makefile sets LDLIBPTH = LIBRARY_PATH=<build dir>, which it needs
in order to find the freshly built shared libperl.so (-Duseshrplib). As written it
also hides libroot.so, so every $(MINIPERL) / $(RUN_PERL) / $(RUN_TESTS)
invocation dies with the loader's exit code 3 and no output. The build then fails
at "Failed to build miniperl. Please run make minitest" even though miniperl is a
perfectly good binary -- run it by hand with LIBRARY_PATH unset and it prints its
version happily. Do not chase the compiler; this is an environment defect.

Do NOT simply clear LDLIBPTH: with -Duseshrplib the build genuinely needs the
build directory on the path.

Applies to: input-source-packages/develop/sources/perl-5.42.2-1/perl-5.42.2.recipe
--- a/perl-5.42.2.recipe
+++ b/perl-5.42.2.recipe
@@ -132,6 +132,18 @@
 		-Dusesitecustomize \
 		-de
 
+	# Haiku's runtime_loader *replaces* the default library search path with
+	# LIBRARY_PATH whenever that variable is set -- it returns the value verbatim
+	# and never appends the defaults (src/system/runtime_loader/runtime_loader.cpp,
+	# which carries an upstream TODO about exactly this). perl's Makefile sets
+	# LDLIBPTH = LIBRARY_PATH=<build dir>, which is needed to find the freshly
+	# built shared libperl.so, but as written it also hides libroot.so, so every
+	# $(MINIPERL) / $(RUN_PERL) / $(RUN_TESTS) invocation dies with the loader's
+	# exit code 3 -- the build fails at "Failed to build miniperl" even though
+	# miniperl itself is fine. Make the path additive instead of exclusive.
+	sed -i -e "s|^\\(LDLIBPTH = LIBRARY_PATH=.*\\)$|\\1:%A/lib:/boot/system/non-packaged/lib:/boot/system/lib|" Makefile
+	grep -n "^LDLIBPTH" Makefile
+
 	# force-remove path from sed
 	sed -i -e 's/LOC_SED\s*"\/bin\/sed/LOC_SED "sed/' config.h
 
